Zstandard (zstd) for OpenVMS
============================

This is Zstandard (zstd) @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified release plus the patches in the vms-zstd project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-zstd

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  Zstandard is free software, dual-licensed under BSD and GPLv2;
see LICENSE and COPYING.  The manual is ZSTD.TXT; ZSTD.1 is the manual page.

What is installed
-----------------
    ZSTD$ROOT:[BIN]ZSTD.EXE
    ZSTD$ROOT:[LIB]LIBZSTD.OLB      the library (libzstd)
    ZSTD$ROOT:[INCLUDE]ZSTD.H, ZSTD_ERRORS.H, ZDICT.H
    ZSTD$ROOT:[DOC]                 this file, LICENSE, COPYING, CHANGELOG
    ZSTD$ROOT:[000000]ZSTD$SETUP.COM   defines the commands
    SYS$STARTUP:ZSTD$STARTUP.COM      defines ZSTD$ROOT

Setting up
----------
The kit installs ZSTD$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define ZSTD$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:ZSTD$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the commands:

    $ @ZSTD$ROOT:[000000]ZSTD$SETUP.COM

Using it
--------
    $ zstd file.txt                 ! file.txt.zst, keeping file.txt
    $ unzstd file.txt.zst           ! zstd -d
    $ zstdcat file.txt.zst          ! to SYS$OUTPUT
    $ zstd "-19" "--rm" file.dat    ! higher level, remove the original

zstd also decodes the old v0.5-v0.7 formats.  A name like file.txt.zst
needs an ODS-5 disk.  This build is single-threaded (no -T).

Text and binary files
---------------------
A VMS text file (variable-length or VFC records) is compressed as text:
its records become lines ending in LF, as on Unix, so the compressed file
can be unpacked anywhere.  Decompressing gives a Stream_LF file with the
same lines.  Any other file (stream, fixed-length records: executables,
kits, binary data) is compressed byte for byte.

Using the library
-----------------
Compile with /INCLUDE=ZSTD$ROOT:[INCLUDE] and link with
ZSTD$ROOT:[LIB]LIBZSTD.OLB/LIBRARY.  The library is compiled /NAMES=(AS_IS,SHORTENED); its
header declares the API so, so a program compiled with any /NAMES links
with it.

Exit status
-----------
Under DCL a failed run exits with an error-severity status, so ON ERROR and
IF .NOT. $STATUS work; the exit code N is ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8.  Under
a GNV shell, $? is the exit code as on Unix.

Upper-case options and batch jobs
---------------------------------
Under the TRADITIONAL DCL parse style DCL upper-cases the unquoted parts of
a command and the C run-time library passes them to the program in lower
case.  Batch jobs use TRADITIONAL even when interactive logins are set to
EXTENDED.  Quote options with capitals, use the long forms, or start the
procedure with

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED
